  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n- Near-critical to critical fire weather expected due to hot, dry\n  and breezy conditions through Saturday.\n\n- Wildfire smoke will continue to impact the region until further\n  notice.\n\nToday through Sunday:\n\nAn upper-level ridge of high pressure will maintain hot, dry \nconditions across western Montana and north-central Idaho into \nearly next week. As the ridge flattens through Saturday, mid- \nlevel flow will increase, yielding stronger afternoon surface \nwinds. The combination of heat, single-digit to low- double-digit \nrelative humidity, gusty winds, and receptive fuels will drive \ncritical fire weather conditions. Red Flag Warnings remain active,\nwith peak concerns focused across southwest and west-central \nMontana, as well as the Palouse and Hells Canyon regions.\n\nWildfire smoke remains the primary forecast challenge. Strong \novernight valley inversions will trap dense smoke each morning, \nparticularly across northwest Montana. While afternoon solar \nheating will promote boundary layer mixing and temporarily improve\nsurface visibility, increased winds will simultaneously \ninvigorate fire activity and generate smoke aloft. Notably, \npersistent smoke cover may attenuate solar radiation and suppress \natmospheric mixing, locally capping maximum temperatures and wind \ngusts below current guidance.\n\nSubtle monsoonal moisture advecting from the south will introduce\na low chance of isolated thunderstorms Saturday afternoon, mainly\nacross southwest Montana and southern Lemhi County. Due to an \nexceptionally dry sub-cloud layer, rainfall will largely evaporate\nas virga, creating a high risk for dry lightning ignitions and \nsudden, erratic microburst gusts between 40 and 55 mph.\n\nMonday through Next Friday:\n\nConfidence is gradually increasing that mid-level moisture will \nsettle in by mid-to-late next week, sustaining periodic \nthunderstorm chances rather than producing widespread, soaking \nprecipitation. Higher confidence exists for temperatures \nmoderating back toward seasonal normals in the mid-to-upper 80s. A\nmajor weather pattern change, such as a strong, deep storm system\nfrom the Pacific Northwest, is necessary to significantly \nsuppress fire activity and clear the smoke. Current forecasts show\nonly a 20 to 30 percent chance of such a system arriving by the \nmiddle to late portions of next week.",
